{"id":"e8a45050-bf46-4a2f-9152-24dc318fa630","article_key":"doi:10.1038/s41562-026-02525-1","title":"Defining collectivism as duties in relationships rather than generalized warmth improves predictive validity in 100 cultures","title_zh":"将集体主义定义为关系中的责任而非普遍温暖可提升100种文化的预测效度","doi":"10.1038/s41562-026-02525-1","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-026-02525-1","abstract":"Abstract Experts widely agree that East Asian cultures are more collectivistic than Western cultures. Yet 76 survey studies found no reliable difference. Our explanation is that the most-used surveys are contaminated with ‘warm fuzzy’ items, which describe warmth and prosociality towards everyone near and far. We argue that collectivism is better understood as ‘responsibilism’, which emphasizes responsibilities in close relationships. A survey found that many cultural psychologists expect collectivistic cultures to agree with warm fuzzy items. Then we developed a responsibilism scale and tested 12,247 people in 100 cultures. Results showed large cultural differences ( d = 3.98 [2.50, 5.43]) that were previously obscured by flawed measurement. Responsibilism predicted differences across societies (such as family ties and corruption) and individuals (social class and politics) that most earlier scales missed. This study offers a solution to previous puzzling findings by distinguishing warm fuzzy prosociality from responsibilism and introducing a scale with stronger predictive validity.","abstract_zh":"摘要：专家普遍认为东亚文化比西方文化更具集体主义倾向。然而，76项调查研究未发现可靠差异。我们认为，最常用的调查工具受‘温暖模糊’项目的污染，这些项目描述了对近处和远处所有人的温暖与亲社会行为。我们主张将集体主义更好地理解为‘责任主义’，强调在亲密关系中的责任。一次调查发现，许多文化心理学家预期集体主义文化会同意温暖模糊项目。随后我们开发了责任主义量表，并在12,247人、100种文化中进行测试。结果显示，文化差异显著（d = 3.98 [2.50, 5.43]），此前被测量缺陷掩盖。责任主义能够预测跨社会（如家庭纽带与腐败）和个体（社会阶层与政治）层面的差异，而早期量表大多未能捕捉到这些差异。本研究通过区分温暖模糊的亲社会性与责任主义，并引入具有更强预测效度的量表，为以往令人困惑的发现提供了解决方案。","snippet":"Abstract Experts widely agree that East Asian cultures are more collectivistic than Western cultures. Yet 76 survey studies found no reliable difference. Our explanation is that the most-used surveys are contaminated with ‘warm fuzzy’ items, which describe warmth and prosociality towards everyone near and far. We argue that collectivism is better understood as ‘responsibilism’, which emphasizes responsibilities in c…","translated_at":"2026-09-15T22:31:49.880893+00:00","translation_model":"openai/gpt-oss-20b","source_category":"doi","article_type":"journal-article","volume":null,"issue":null,"published_at":"2026-09-15T00:00:00","acquired_at":"2026-09-15T20:08:37Z","display_date":"2026-09-15T20:08:37Z","display_date_source":"acquired","online_published_at":"2026-09-15T00:00:00","print_published_at":null,"first_author":"Thomas Talhelm","authors_text":"Thomas Talhelm, Liuqing Wei, Rui Sun, Danila Medvedev, Álvaro San Martin, May Helmy, Adil Samekin, Alejandra Zaragoza Scherman, Alexander S. English, The Responsibilism Collaboration Team, Andreea Ursu, Séamus A. Power, Chia-Wen Chen, Qionghan Zhang, Ali H. Al-Hoorie, Annabella Osei-Tutu, Laura Vuillier, Katija Khan, Amanda Atwood, Luxi Shen, Muhammad Rizwan, Serigne Abdou Lahat Ndiaye, Oumar Barry, Tengku Nila Fadhlia, Igor Grossmann, Jordan R. Axt, Nicholas Calbraith Owsley, Salome Njambi, Illia Yahiiaiev, Karine Malysheva, Serhii Lytvyn, Irena Sarieva, Xuemin Zhang, Chen Zhao, Robert Ritz, BoKyung Park, Maria Constantinou, Mário José Chanja, Syeda Arzu Wasti, Synne Marit Kjelling Skagseth, Jonas R. Kunst, Tuong Van Vu, Isaraphorn Pratumchai, Phakkanun Chittham, Narendra Singh Thagunna, Jyotshna Dangi, Tulips Yiwen Wang, Vassilis Pavlopoulos, Michal Kohút, Freyr Halldorsson, Deborah Shulman, Anita Deak, Watcharaporn Boonyasiriwat, Bojana Većkalov, Ricardo B. Rodrigues, Rui Costa-Lopes, Patricia Gale, William J. Chopik, Lusanda Sekaja, Orla Muldoon, Donna-Maria B. Maynard, Ashley Foster-Estwick, Amy H. Du, Rabiah Aminudin, Edna Kabala, Marcus Eugênio Oliveira Lima, Eunkook M. Suh, Michael Muthukrishna, Jinseok P. Kim, Kazi Nur Hossain, Arobindu Dash, Stefan A